  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe environment today looks similar to yesterday's. However, \ndeep layer shear is forecast to be ever-so-slightly higher,\nwhich may allow a slightly improved storm organization \npotential and perhaps a slightly better chance of a few storms \nreaching severe, or near- severe, levels. The primary risk \nshould be strong winds, but perhaps some hail can occur in the \ntallest, most sustained, cores. The focus for convection should \nbe the seabreeze and an inland trough. The low-level flow may \nveer to southwesterly, helping keep the seabreeze less \nprogressive compared to today, and storms may not be as \nprogressive. With a high PWAT airmass still in place, this could\nallow a slight bump up in the risk for urban and poor drainage \nflooding, with at least a low-end risk of flash flooding. The\nbest chance for storms appear to be east of hwy 17 closer to the\ncoast, where instability will be the highest. Further inland we\nshould remain relatively stable, reducing shower/tstorm chances. \n\nFinally, a cold front will sweep south through the area on \nMonday. Guidance continues to trend on the slower side with the \npassage, and now this sets up the potential for the front to \nmove through in the afternoon/evening hours. This will be \nsomething to watch as deep layer shear is forecast to be higher \n(~30kt), and may support a strong/severe thunderstorm risk \nconditional on sufficient instability being realized. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level ridging quickly rebuilds across the\nSouthern U.S. next week. Meanwhile, a notably strong surface \nhigh will nose south along the lee of the Appalachians. \nSynoptically this is a very dry pattern for the region. Despite \nwarming low-level thicknesses beneath the ridge, the \nnortheasterly flow around the high should help to hold temps \ndown some. Additionally, the airmass will be drier with less \nhumidity, making it feel not as hot. It will feel a little more \nlike fall, especially at night and in the morning.",
